# Plates, crust and elevation

The generator simulates tectonics. `tectonics.js` holds the whole model and is
free of WebGL and DOM so it can be measured headlessly with `bun run stats`.
Run that after any change to the model and check the numbers against Earth
before judging the pictures.

## The rule that matters

**Coasts come from interpolation over a continuous field, never from plate
outlines.** This is the one thing that must not regress. The generator once
grew land as blobs that filled a plate, and the result was coastlines that
traced plate boundaries.

Today the coastline is the sea-level contour of a continuous elevation field
built from crustal thickness and sea-floor age, relaxed across the margin
(`coastBlend`). Crust type is seeded from a noise field that knows nothing
about the plate partition, so a plate can carry a continent and an ocean at
once. Keep it that way.

Do not:

- Grow continents as blobs on plates, or derive crust type from plate id
- Floor continental plates or flatten ocean basins to a constant depth
- `Math.max(elevation, …)` on a plate or a cell
- Widen `coastBlend`: a wide blend averages a margin against the abyssal plain
  and drowns it

## The model

Three layers, each measurable:

1. **Plate geometry.** Weighted competitive growth, with plate areas drawn from
   two populations — a few majors holding ~90% of the surface, then a
   microplate tail. Tuned to Earth: top-1 18% (Earth 20%), top-3 49% (49%),
   top-7 89% (90%). A uniform flood fill gives every plate the same expected
   area and reads as interchangeable blobs.

2. **Motion.** Rigid rotation about an Euler pole, `omega x r`, in a
   no-net-rotation frame. Not one constant vector per plate: a single 3D vector
   is tangent at one point only, and on 20 plates it puts >30% of the velocity
   into the radial direction across a third of the surface. Boundaries are
   classified convergent / divergent / transform from the relative velocity
   against the boundary normal, and sub-classified by crust pair.

3. **Simulation.** The crust is the state: type, age, thickness, orogeny, arc.
   Each step the plates rotate and carry their crust with them (semi-Lagrangian,
   by back-rotating each cell and asking which plate covers it). Gaps at
   divergent margins stretch the neighbouring margin thin and then break to new
   sea floor at age zero; convergence subducts the denser side and records an
   arc or thickens the crust; sustained continental collision welds plates.
   `healPlates` keeps ownership coherent — without it, twenty steps of cell-wise
   advection shred the map into interpenetrating slivers.

Elevation is read off the crust: ocean depth from half-space cooling
(`5650 - 3050·exp(-age/62.8)` metres), land height from Airy isostasy on
thickness (145 m per km, relative to `seaLevelThicknessKm`).

`seaLevelThicknessKm` is the land-fraction dial — it stands for how much water
the planet has, and is deliberately separate from `crustReferenceKm`, the
thickness undisturbed crust relaxes towards.

## The 1843 path

The original distance-field blend (`findCollisions` + `assignRegionElevation`)
is still there behind the **Simulate tectonics** toggle, off means 1843, and
`bun run preview --no-tectonics`. Keep it working as a comparison. It is no
longer bit-identical to the original: it is fed plate velocities taken at each
plate's centroid rather than the old random neighbour-direction vectors, so
that the codebase has one motion model. Its `<` compression pick and collision
table are otherwise untouched.

`mergeOceanPlates` and `connectWorldOcean` remain UI toggles, both off by
default. `mergeOceanPlates` only applies on the 1843 path.

## After a change

Capture and **read** the images — code alone does not tell you whether it
worked:

```
bun run stats                         # numbers first
bun run preview                       # globe + equirect
bun run preview plates                # plate shapes, sizes, motion
bun run preview crust                 # sea-floor age, orogeny, boundary types
```

`crust` is the view that shows what the simulation is actually doing: ridges
with young pale crust beside them fading to dark old crust, trenches, and
transform segments.
